Turn on the bootstrap traces that are already written, and route them to the
console.

xnu carries a labelled trace of its own startup in three places, and all three
are commented out:

    kernel_bootstrap_log()            osfmk/kern/startup.c
    kernel_bootstrap_thread_log()     osfmk/kern/startup.c
    bsd_init_kprintf()                bsd/kern/bsd_init.c

What is left behind when they are disabled is kernel_debug_string_early(),
which writes into the ktrace buffer. That is useful on a machine you can
attach to afterwards and useless on one whose only output is a panel.

This matters because the stretch between PE_init_iokit() and bsd_init() is
otherwise completely silent. IOKit prints nothing on its own unless the "io"
boot-arg raises gIOKitDebug, and the first thing bsd_init() prints is the
copyright banner, so a kernel that stops anywhere in between looks exactly
like a kernel that finished booting and has nothing to say. These calls name
every step of it.

They are turned on as printf rather than the kprintf they were written as.
kprintf goes to PE_kputc, which is the serial port -- and a board without one
leaves it pointing at the console, needs debug=0x8 to be unmuted at all, and
loses everything printed before vcattach(), which clears the screen and
replays only the message buffer. printf goes through the message buffer, so it
survives that replay and needs no boot-arg. The ktrace call is left in place in
both functions.

Turning bsd_init_kprintf() on exposes a latent syntax error a few hundred
lines further down:

    bsd_init_kprintf("calling sysctl_mib_init\n");
    sysctl_mib_init()

    bsd_init_kprintf("calling bsd_autoconf\n");

sysctl_mib_init() has no terminator. While the macro expands to nothing, the
semicolon written after the *next* bsd_init_kprintf() ends up terminating it,
and the file compiles. Give the macro a body and that semicolon belongs to the
printf instead, leaving the call dangling. So the trace cannot ever have been
switched on in this tree. The missing semicolon is added here. It is the only
statement in either file that depends on the trick -- the rest were checked.

Two stretches had no trace points of their own and have been given some:
StartIOKit(), and the run from PE_init_iokit() returning to bsd_init(). Those
are the last unlit parts of the boot, and between them sits everything that
happens once IOKit exists -- the kext bootstrap being jettisoned, the commpage
being mapped, kernel text protections being finalised. A kernel that stops in
there prints its last line from inside PE_init_iokit and says nothing further,
which is indistinguishable from a kernel that finished.

commpage_populate() is traced too, because that is where this port currently
stops: vm_shared_region_init() returns, vm_commpage_init() is entered and never
comes back, and on arm that function is little more than a call to
commpage_populate(). The steps inside it reach in several directions -- the
pmap, the cpuid tables, the clock -- so it is worth naming each one rather than
guessing which.

This is a debugging aid. It is noisy by design and belongs in a bring-up tree,
not a shipping one.

The whole trace is switchable, and that is worth a paragraph because it was
not at first, and then was only half so. The console here is a framebuffer
with no scrollback. These are around a hundred and ten lines -- forty in
bsd_init(), the rest in kernel_bootstrap(), commpage and StartIOKit -- and
they sit directly above everything that happens afterwards. That was free
while the boot stopped inside bsd_init(). It stopped being free once the
root filesystem mounted and the interesting output moved past them: fsck's
verdict on the root volume arrives long after bsd_init() is done, and four
attempts to photograph it caught the trace instead. The first fix switched
only bsd_init()'s share, which freed forty lines and was still not enough --
the other seventy were the ones at the top of the photograph.

boottrace=0 turns all four off. bsdtrace is the name the flag had while it
covered bsd_init() alone and is still accepted, because that is what is
written in the command lines this tree has been booted with. The default is
on: for the stretch it covers it is still the only thing that says where a
silent kernel stopped.

One flag, defined in osfmk/kern/startup.c and read by the other three.
kernel_bootstrap() traces first, so it is the one that reads the boot-arg,
lazily, on its first line; the others only test the flag. It starts at -1,
which is truthy, so a caller that somehow arrives before the boot-arg has
been looked at still prints rather than silently not. IOStartIOKit.cpp
declares it extern "C", since startup.c is C and the symbol it defines is
unmangled.
--- a/osfmk/kern/startup.c
+++ b/osfmk/kern/startup.c
@@ -228,17 +228,55 @@
 /* mach leak logging */
 int log_leaks = 0;
 
+/*
+ * Bring-up: printf, not the kprintf these lines used to be, and switchable.
+ * See the bootstrap-trace patch header.
+ *
+ * The flag lives here because kernel_bootstrap() is the first of the four
+ * places that trace, and the other three -- commpage, StartIOKit, bsd_init
+ * -- read it rather than each parsing the boot-arg again. It starts at -1,
+ * which is truthy, so a caller that somehow arrives before the boot-arg has
+ * been looked at still prints.
+ *
+ * boottrace=0 turns the whole trace off. bsdtrace is the name this had when
+ * it only covered bsd_init() and is still accepted, because that is what is
+ * written in the command lines this tree has been booted with.
+ */
+int bootstrap_trace = -1;
+
+static void
+bootstrap_trace_read_boot_arg(void)
+{
+	uint32_t on = 1;
+
+	if (!PE_parse_boot_argn("boottrace", &on, sizeof(on))) {
+		on = 1;
+		(void)PE_parse_boot_argn("bsdtrace", &on, sizeof(on));
+	}
+	bootstrap_trace = (int)on;
+}
+
 static inline void
 kernel_bootstrap_log(const char *message)
 {
-//	kprintf("kernel_bootstrap: %s\n", message);
+	if (bootstrap_trace < 0) {
+		bootstrap_trace_read_boot_arg();
+	}
+	if (bootstrap_trace) {
+		printf("kernel_bootstrap: %s\n", message);
+	}
 	kernel_debug_string_early(message);
 }
 
